- The six beats already run in the right day-order (riders → tack room → flock → pup → dusk), but weight is misallocated: for this tradition the stirrup beat should be the fulcrum everything else is lit by, and instead it's one beat among six, explained once, then forgotten until a single closing simile.
- The ending stacks three climaxes back to back — pup's graduation, the dogs-teaching-dogs correction, and the whisperer's exit — with no clear summit. "The last lesson is the gate" wants one peak; the correction scene should read as evidence gathered along the way, not a competing finale.
- "Dogs teaching dogs" plus "the kennel pays itself" nearly restates Vol I's own closing thesis rather than climbing past it. Vol I already earned "the dogs whisper to each other" — Vol II needs the next rung: not peer correction, but a lineage so many generations deep that no human hand remains anywhere in the chain, not even the whisperer's.
- The stirrup is said once by the old-timer, then said again as an explicit simile at the exit ("the way a stirrup rides in a saddle"). It should go underground after the tack room and resurface only as unnamed physical detail — a stance, a way of setting weight forward — so the reader completes the image instead of being told it twice.
- The whisperer isn't yet built as the vestige herself. The mythic frame wants her body to carry the old man's shape the way tack carries a dead knight's — give her one inherited physical tell, planted early and unremarked, so her walking away *is* the stirrup riding forward, not a thing compared to it.
- The closing epigraph echoes Vol I's cadence too directly ("whisperer's work is done the day the dogs whisper" → "the teacher becomes the road") — it should rhyme, not restate the thesis; let the final image carry the meaning and keep any closing line short and oblique rather than a second explanation.

---REWRITE---

The boy the father had promised came at last — except he was a father now, and what he carried on his shoulders was a girl, six years old and loud about it.

"Grandpa said the dogs teach themselves," she said.

"They do," her father said. "But somebody had to teach the teaching."

At the ranch house the whisperer was waiting — a woman the old man had started, back when the kennel still needed starting. Riders were coming, and she had a day of work to show the girl.

The jockey came through the gate first, small and light, in a hurry the way water is in a hurry. He wanted fast. The whisperer shook her head. "You want quick. Fast is a road horse; quick is a cow horse." She gave him the little mare that turns like a thought, and the small dog that reads a knee before the rider knows he's asked. "Short rides, short words."

The packer came at noon, a string of mules, a man who thought in miles. She gave him no racer — wide calm horses, big pacing dogs that could walk beside a mule from dark to dark without losing their temper. "Long knots, checked twice. His job isn't to be first. His job is to arrive, all of him, every load."

Last came a kid on a fat patient pony, holding the rope all wrong and having a wonderful time. She matched him to the gentlest dog in the yard, one that had seen every mistake a child could make and thought none of them were news. The pony stood like a hill.

"Quick, steady, patient," her father said, low, by the girl's ear. "The machine world needs all three hands, and not one of them is the best one."

In the tack room, the old-timer waited — the notebook man from the colt years, his thumb still graphite-smudged. He took a stirrup off the wall, wide and flared and heavy, and hung it in front of the girl.

"Knight's stirrup. Built so a man in iron could put his whole weight behind a lance. The knights are four hundred years gone — look at every saddle in this barn, the shape's still riding with you."

"Why keep it?" the girl asked.

"Because it kept somebody alive," he said, "and you're never sure the war is over." He stood while he said it the way he always stood, weight forward on the balls of his feet, like a man still bracing something. In the doorway, the whisperer stood the exact same way, and had never once been told why.

"A token ceiling is somebody's old lance," the father said. "Kept because it held. You don't file it off till you're sure. You're never sure."

The flock came after lunch, and the girl asked the wrong question first. "Which one's the boss sheep?"

"Count again," her father said. Outside the sheep there was a line of command — one whistle, the lead dog taking it, the young dogs taking their line off the lead dog, like an army. Inside the sheep there was nothing of the kind. Pull any ewe out and the flock flowed on. No boss. Only pressure, moving like weather. The dogs stood in the command line on one side and in the weather on the other — the only ones who could.

"The work comes in like sheep," her father said. "No leader, only pressure. You don't herd it by being a bigger sheep. You send dogs."

Late in the day the pup went out, first season, off-leash, the whistle left hanging on the post. She gathered the small band clean, set them at the gate, and lay down. Nobody clapped. Nobody noticed at all — nobody but the old grey bitch, who had been watching the whole thing, and now stopped. Turned away. Found the last patch of sun.

"That's the graduation," the whisperer said. "Not the ribbon. The watcher stopping."

"Nobody told her good dog," the girl said.

"The old dog told her," the whisperer said, "by not telling her anything."

"The finest harness," her father said, "is the one that isn't there anymore."

On the way back the girl saw it, and nobody else did: a young dog cut another's flank, a shoulder check, a growl pitched exactly right — a correction the kennel had never been taught by any human hand. She ran and told the whisperer, expecting news.

The whisperer only nodded, unsurprised. "That dog was corrected the same way once, by a dog I never trained. And her teacher was never trained by me either. Go back far enough in that chain and you run out of human hands to find." She said it plainly, the way you'd report weather.

"Somewhere back down that chain," the father said, quiet, "there's no human hand left at all. That's not a wage anymore. That's a species now."

At dusk the whisperer oiled her boots, whistled up her one old dog, and walked toward the road.

The girl ran after her. "Are you done? Is the kennel finished?"

"No," said the whisperer. "Finished is for museums."

"Then why are you leaving?"

"Because it doesn't whisper to me anymore."

"To who, then?"

She pointed back at the yard, where the amber lights were buzzing on — an old dog showing a young dog where to stand, a young dog showing a smaller one. "Itself."

She went down the road with her weight already forward, balanced on the balls of her feet like she was still bracing something no one else could see, a stance she had never been taught and had never once noticed she carried. The girl stood at the rail a long time, watching her go, without quite knowing what she was watching.

*The old wars ride on in shapes nobody questions anymore — a curve of iron, a woman's stance, a kennel that no longer says her name.*
